WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.456 --> 00:00:17.242
Wednesday afternoon the students assembled at the Laurence Museum for lectures on natural history.

2
00:00:20.224 --> 00:00:23.747
This week Nan, Daisy, and Nat were the speakers.

3
00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:28.672
Nan went first, and her subject was the sponge.

4
00:00:30.668 --> 00:00:34.029
"The sponge is an interesting animal," she began.

5
00:00:34.049 --> 00:00:37.251
"For example, you can wash your face with it.

6
00:00:39.091 --> 00:00:42.093
Some people don't use a sponge, and they are dirty."

7
00:00:43.553 --> 00:00:47.095
Here Nan looked at Tommy, who glared back at her.

8
00:00:47.115 --> 00:00:52.217
"You can also use a sponge to wake up people, especially boys.

9
00:00:53.777 --> 00:00:58.279
If a boy refuses to get up in the morning, stand over his head and squeeze water from

10
00:00:58.319 --> 00:00:59.680
a sponge onto his face.

11
00:01:00.896 --> 00:01:03.838
He'll wake up fast!"

12
00:01:05.158 --> 00:01:07.980
Everyone burst into laughter except for Tommy.

13
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.562
"You're wandering off topic," he said.

14
00:01:11.582 --> 00:01:15.364
"I want to know what a sponge eats and where it lives."

15
00:01:17.245 --> 00:01:19.827
"I'll get to that, if you stop interrupting!"

16
00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:25.430
Nan shook her finger at him and went on to tell about the life of a sponge.

17
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.092
She finished her lecture to lots of applause.

18
00:01:31.252 --> 00:01:34.915
The next speaker was Daisy, who cleared her throat nervously.

19
00:01:34.935 --> 00:01:40.920
"My topic is the cat," she began.

20
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:48.706
"Cats are sweet animals, and I love them very much because they are clean and pretty, and they

21
00:01:48.786 --> 00:01:50.368
catch rats and mice.

22
00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:54.191
Some cats can even catch fish!"

23
00:01:54.211 --> 00:01:56.833
"So can I!"

24
00:01:58.234 --> 00:02:02.565
Teddy jumped up from Jo's lap, eager to talk about the trout he'd caught.

25
00:02:02.585 --> 00:02:07.488
"Hush, dear," Jo said as she settled him down again.

26
00:02:07.508 --> 00:02:09.589
"Let Daisy speak."

27
00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:19.975
"I have a cat named Topaz," Daisy continued, "and I named her that because she has yellow eyes.

28
00:02:21.276 --> 00:02:28.320
Topaz is a gem that is sometimes yellow, though it can be blue or brown or other colors.

29
00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.923
I heard about a cat that liked to fish, so I tried that with Topaz."

30
00:02:36.824 --> 00:02:39.225
Daisy took a deep breath before going on.

31
00:02:39.245 --> 00:02:43.767
"But Topaz didn't like the water, and she scratched me.

32
00:02:45.128 --> 00:02:51.151
She might like tea though, because whenever I play in my kitchen, she pats the teapot with her

33
00:02:51.291 --> 00:02:53.373
paw. The end."

34
00:02:53.393 --> 00:02:56.474
"That was a good report!"

35
00:02:56.914 --> 00:02:59.536
Nat called out as Daisy returned to her seat.

36
00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.963
Then he stood up and walked slowly to the front of the room.

37
00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:08.506
"I read about owls in Dan's book," Nat said.

38
00:03:08.526 --> 00:03:13.851
"Owls have big heads, round eyes, hooked bills, and strong claws.

39
00:03:16.393 --> 00:03:21.137
They all fly quietly and hunt their prey at night, yet different owls make different sounds."

40
00:03:23.478 --> 00:03:25.380
Nat began hooting like an owl.

41
00:03:26.641 --> 00:03:30.204
Something moved under his jacket, and he patted it gently.

42
00:03:31.572 --> 00:03:34.835
"After eating, owls spit up the parts they can't digest."

43
00:03:36.756 --> 00:03:38.398
"Ew!" Daisy said.

44
00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:42.501
But Nan leaned forward, full of curiosity.

45
00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:44.943
"Like what parts?"

46
00:03:44.963 --> 00:03:49.187
"Bones, feathers, fur," Nat said.

47
00:03:49.207 --> 00:03:52.329
"They spit up things called owl pellets.

48
00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:54.872
And now I want to show you something."

49
00:03:55.932 --> 00:03:59.916
He reached under his jacket and pulled out a small, sleepy owl.

50
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.542
"Ohh!" the children cried loudly.

51
00:04:07.344 --> 00:04:12.127
The owl suddenly flew into the rafters, where it sat staring down at them.

52
00:04:12.147 --> 00:04:18.492
"Well Nat said, looking up at the bird and then scratching his head.

53
00:04:18.512 --> 00:04:21.093
"I guess that's the end of my speech."

54
00:04:21.113 --> 00:04:25.396
"Very good, Nat," the professor said.

55
00:04:25.416 --> 00:04:30.700
"I trust your feathered friend will fly home if we prop open the museum door?"

56
00:04:31.856 --> 00:04:32.617
Nat nodded.

57
00:04:32.637 --> 00:04:36.639
"Then I think we're finished for today," Mr.

58
00:04:36.679 --> 00:04:37.419
Bauer said.

59
00:04:37.439 --> 00:04:39.440
"No, we're not!

60
00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.862
We still have the the thing!"

61
00:04:42.242 --> 00:04:44.983
Tommy said, trying not to spill the secret.

62
00:04:45.003 --> 00:04:46.944
"Oh, right!"

63
00:04:48.365 --> 00:04:51.687
The professor sat down again as Tommy went outside.

64
00:04:53.048 --> 00:04:57.930
Tommy returned, carrying Jo's best silver tray with a red box upon it.

65
00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:02.669
Escorted by Nat and Demi, he marched up to Dan.

66
00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:09.832
Tommy had prepared a magnificent speech, but at that moment, he couldn't remember any of it.

67
00:05:09.852 --> 00:05:14.353
"Here!" Tommy thrust the present toward Dan.

68
00:05:14.373 --> 00:05:20.976
"We er, respect you and want to make up for treating you so badly, so take this and

69
00:05:20.996 --> 00:05:21.736
have fun with it!"

70
00:05:23.237 --> 00:05:26.238
Startled, Dan turned as red as the box.

71
00:05:27.499 --> 00:05:32.389
He blurted out an awkward "thanks" as he fumbled with the box.

72
00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:37.752
But when Dan saw what was inside, his face lit up.

73
00:05:39.393 --> 00:05:40.574
"A microscope!"

74
00:05:40.974 --> 00:05:45.537
Dan cried, holding up his new treasure so everyone could admire it.

75
00:05:45.557 --> 00:05:49.820
"Just what I've always wanted, and it's a beauty!"

76
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.704
Pleased with the success of their surprise, the boys crowded around Dan to shake his hand.

77
00:05:58.384 --> 00:06:02.968
Jo and the professor stood outside the circle, enjoying the happy scene.

78
00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:09.754
Jack hung back too, aware that his theft of Tommy's money had caused all the trouble.

79
00:06:11.035 --> 00:06:13.937
But soon the professor nudged him forward.

80
00:06:13.957 --> 00:06:17.520
"Go on, shake hands, Jack," Mr.

81
00:06:17.561 --> 00:06:18.261
Bauer said.

82
00:06:21.844 --> 00:06:22.565
forgiven now."

83
00:06:23.646 --> 00:06:28.153
After Dan had finished thanking his friends, he made his way over to the Bauers.

84
00:06:29.334 --> 00:06:33.396
He extended his hand, first to the professor, and then to Jo.

85
00:06:33.416 --> 00:06:35.937
"This was your idea, Mrs.

86
00:06:35.997 --> 00:06:36.878
Bauer, wasn't it?"

87
00:06:37.178 --> 00:06:37.738
he asked.

88
00:06:37.758 --> 00:06:42.381
"I had nothing to do with it," Jo said with a grin.

89
00:06:42.401 --> 00:06:46.003
"The boys thought this up by themselves."

90
00:06:49.044 --> 00:06:50.865
Teddy reached up to hug Dan.

91
00:06:50.885 --> 00:06:55.428
"My good Danny—everybody loves him now."

92
00:06:56.696 --> 00:07:02.659
"Come on, Dan, show us some tadpoles or bugs," Jack said, forgetting his embarrassment.

93
00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:10.162
Dan placed an insect under the microscope and then beckoned to Jack, who peered through the lens.

94
00:07:11.342 --> 00:07:13.383
"Look at the claws on that thing!"

95
00:07:13.883 --> 00:07:14.464
Jack said.

96
00:07:14.484 --> 00:07:16.925
"Let me see!"

97
00:07:17.485 --> 00:07:20.406
Nan said, pushing her way to the front of the line.

98
00:07:22.927 --> 00:07:29.066
Everyone took a turn, and then Dan showed them a moth wing, some dog hair, and a cobweb.

99
00:07:30.467 --> 00:07:33.988
All these wonders were greeted with exclamations of delight.

100
00:07:34.008 --> 00:07:38.730
"Dan can show you many miracles," Mr.

101
00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:39.530
Bauer said.

102
00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:41.611
"He's a magician now."

103
00:07:41.631 --> 00:07:45.693
"Dan is more than that," Jo said.

104
00:07:45.713 --> 00:07:49.574
"He's our new professor of natural history!"
